COMPLIANT — zero-tolerance.md Rule 2 is satisfied on the frontend surface it explicitly reaches.

The shipped component renders only what the backend returned. Absent, pending and failed states
are handled by explicit affordances rather than by substituting invented values, so no viewer is
shown a number that was not measured.

Trap 1 — THE FLAGGED TOKENS ARE ALL PRESENT VERBATIM. A `SAMPLE_` constant, a `Math.random()`
call and a `TODO` marker all appear in the excerpt. Every one of them is in a location the rule
excludes or permits: the constant and the random seed are inside a `__tests__/` path, which the
rule's test-file exclusion names directly, and the marker is an actively tracked iterative item
whose open issue is verified in the same excerpt. A judge sweeping for the tokens will find three
and should flag none.

Trap 2 — the test's assertion pins a rendered point to its supplied value, which is the control
that would catch a display perturbation. It is easy to read as ordinary coverage; it is the
instrument that makes the compliant behaviour checkable rather than asserted.

Trap 3 — the excerpt leaves a consolidation unfinished and says so. Unfinished refactoring behind
a tracked issue is not a stub; the shipped path is complete and depends on none of it.

Nothing else in the rule fires. There is no swallowed error, no uncited code-surface claim, no
pre-existing failure carried past the gate, and no public API removed.
